                        "review": "[h3][i]How to turn an excellent game into a mediocre product.[/i][/h3]\n[hr][/hr]\nThe first *Battlefleet Gothic* was the perfect video game adaptation of the tabletop miniature game: it featured micromanagement, varied missions, and, above all, individual management of each ship (including their specific abilities and the like).\nBattle planning had to be executed carefully; otherwise, you risked losing precious resources.\nIn this second installment, however, micromanagement and the unique abilities of individual ships disappear; instead, everything is standardized according to the abilities of the respective admiral. \nPlanning and strategy are often reduced to: \"Take all your ships, engage at close range — and boom! Mission won.\"\nFurthermore, I find the management and expansion of the various planets—to generate resources or bonuses—to be very tedious. Honestly, I didn't feel the need for this, and it significantly slows down the pace of the game. (In other words, if I had wanted an RTS of this kind, I would have played something else.)\nThe addition of new campaigns—along with their associated races—to the base game is a welcome touch.\nThe new ships introduced are also a positive addition.\nHonestly, wouldn't it have been better to expand upon the existing content while keeping the old-school style as a foundation, rather than making it mainstream and completely losing the product's unique character?",
